[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d4b5f4-4597-5fb2-8661-87271390504e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486634} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f2da0d-8ced-511f-9af3-03d20168e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486968} {:answer/id #uuid "af82d238-ad19-5d0c-84ab-db2ccd88c886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486987} {:answer/id #uuid "f733835b-7fab-5fb8-8f67-caf01a296a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487122} {:answer/id #uuid "faee4b2e-60b6-5fd3-8b48-4fdb4f844b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f733835b-7fab-5fb8-8f67-caf01a296a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487122} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaed71bb-0e77-5fb5-88f5-a08c5de3d01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e726e2b9-ad90-5a69-bca6-00fc64c93333" :question/math-academy-id "q-89549" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7b7700-3e95-510c-a153-82350219ac3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486620} {:answer/id #uuid "65aa0c48-3279-5f91-ae4c-1449b2527192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486818} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad51fd-d8f5-59d7-8e16-ad0e1d154496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487020} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487059} {:answer/id #uuid "de845076-a6ce-55d2-9cd1-cde3a48b2d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487059} :answer-field/id #uuid "73153ef3-6520-50a9-82e3-ddb42b9894a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd2b3866-cbeb-5f5a-bcbc-008f4c1cdcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253912" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/8b/8be6d8824c8cbf1fb66d64d3ccc027f2949149adc9f95cf4d1f3a814baec80b3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $-1.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-1\\notin A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3ff14c-bd71-5edd-9ce3-7a15e330b984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3ff14c-bd71-5edd-9ce3-7a15e330b984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487462} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbbff3c0-fd43-59f0-8b75-8d882b1ddde3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06dba1a6-575d-58cc-96f9-bc295e858086" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=e^{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(e^{x})}{(e^{x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7eed3b-6e94-58ff-a735-b8d7429d5a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7eed3b-6e94-58ff-a735-b8d7429d5a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487463} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ec5f5b-1856-5a8b-a828-90b43d0da5df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bad9e9f-685f-504c-b1b2-46b994dcd8dd" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{9 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{9 x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{9 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=9 e^{9 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{9 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(9 e^{9 x})}{(e^{9 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(9 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 9 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da5edfb5-96f8-5e7d-89f2-9f743bf0bb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da5edfb5-96f8-5e7d-89f2-9f743bf0bb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487466} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9ca27b6-6a71-5e3d-abb9-6924714b214c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43481be6-03be-562b-89a5-7c8695090e89" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{3 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{3 x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{3 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=3 e^{3 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{3 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(3 e^{3 x})}{(e^{3 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 3 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ef752fe-c8e7-5714-85a9-0630780ed5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef752fe-c8e7-5714-85a9-0630780ed5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487461} :answer-field/id #uuid "be083d33-9396-5f2d-8f6c-2365e427f22c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be93be3a-f700-523a-abbb-8f17fed03030" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{4 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{4 x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{4 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=4 e^{4 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{4 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(4 e^{4 x})}{(e^{4 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 4 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a21799-c311-5f42-890e-d2bbef261f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487700} {:answer/id #uuid "4664d354-13a3-5b8f-8c42-976b3bfeddab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487703} {:answer/id #uuid "69d9183c-bdff-521b-b708-8cb79202331f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487778} {:answer/id #uuid "9d12f52e-2750-5d73-b590-8503bc8f2241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487859} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f051b2-54c1-5939-9ae0-73a9731038be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d9183c-bdff-521b-b708-8cb79202331f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487778} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a7042c-7e1e-5fcb-b886-9667e5b24c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20bd28d6-3021-5539-a5f9-9ec2d307a221" :question/math-academy-id "q-61716" :question/problem "If $h(t)=128\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{t},$ then $h(3)=$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate $h(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $h(t).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =128\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =128\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =128\\cdot \\frac{1}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{64} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e65e89-b6a1-56e9-bd5e-da67acd6079c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487569} {:answer/id #uuid "1f6777e1-b57b-5667-bba3-1a26c27e63e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487633} {:answer/id #uuid "59872684-ab61-548c-a315-d38040730c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487747} {:answer/id #uuid "c5842798-b8d9-5edc-85f3-9c23e26a0409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487942} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b05117-82ad-54b2-b2bb-0dd42a0b1a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b05117-82ad-54b2-b2bb-0dd42a0b1a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488019} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5318616-df05-5cb7-9c1a-585554d2944f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "654ece2b-abda-569c-8738-da98c426c46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142891" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{9}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(2\\,|\\,3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(2\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=2)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,2)+f(3,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(3,2)=\\frac{9}{20}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $f(3,2)=\\frac{9}{20}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(2\\,|\\,3) & =P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{20})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a207cf-6494-5af2-b52a-41f556e25100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487606} {:answer/id #uuid "14ac090f-06af-58f6-a876-572a7a915072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487611} {:answer/id #uuid "818f48fd-f209-588b-b57a-55e82b90bb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487815} {:answer/id #uuid "91ec9b4b-73ba-5016-a495-db90de87e967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487843} {:answer/id #uuid "eb46e060-8079-5761-b929-c7bcbfacd690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb46e060-8079-5761-b929-c7bcbfacd690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488008} :answer-field/id #uuid "69942c77-cdc5-573d-a72f-6608a052a092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c97061a8-e888-5c8e-ae09-c8d25cf5d2a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-111930" :question/problem "If $f(t)=A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}$ and $f(3)=4,$ then $A=$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=3$ and $f(t)=4$ into the expression for $f(t)$ and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\ 4\\cdot 125 & =A \\\\ 500 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=500.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424cb1f4-7e81-5741-9fea-91a70c42cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{13}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488607} {:answer/id #uuid "4c403958-65ff-5e3d-a88d-eb4c593bbb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=7\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488618} {:answer/id #uuid "590bf0de-aa9c-5cf8-b6b6-eb31e0700ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488640} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d0eb3a-5dc4-5b58-944e-b28df01e747b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=7\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488807} {:answer/id #uuid "b98431df-8366-56c8-bb4a-a0335b0aa79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{13}{2}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c403958-65ff-5e3d-a88d-eb4c593bbb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=7\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488618} :answer-field/id #uuid "11588f66-7282-571e-8e23-97b1f1c241fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03070e45-f0e3-5991-b98c-709745709961" :question/math-academy-id "q-253526" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/ca/cae6f56db55b49b98df6ce7e3ae0777e725af14de9dedb5b61b54039bac41860.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $7.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 7$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<7$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<7.$\n\nTherefore, $[7,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[7,\\infty )=7.\n$$\n\nNotice that $7$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=7\\notin A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fd20a8-7d5e-59b1-a20f-b74ee738720b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37fd20a8-7d5e-59b1-a20f-b74ee738720b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9febe4e4-9bba-517f-959d-bd5f66e0861c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4a8742a-2daa-546c-b4d9-fbc8f9111dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4a8742a-2daa-546c-b4d9-fbc8f9111dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488832} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f64c01-1af9-5569-9108-05d2c71dd293" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24cf9a94-daed-5188-bbde-60124c7a3e18" :question/math-academy-id "q-181189" :question/problem "The solution to the inequality $-\\frac{y}{3}\\le 4$ is $y${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,4 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge -12 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}3)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -12 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & \\ge -12 \\\\ y & \\ge -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge -12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27055c91-5496-54a5-a484-9ee0db9c9232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le -32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488551} {:answer/id #uuid "74fe5fa6-f788-5018-bdad-67fe0261dcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge 32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488701} {:answer/id #uuid "a634d207-164b-5687-8998-961c7b33c805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488801} {:answer/id #uuid "b37e6a67-494b-57c7-b107-a4d9f4fb48d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488829} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fa7825-9607-55f9-afee-7c78dcee3ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b37e6a67-494b-57c7-b107-a4d9f4fb48d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488829} :answer-field/id #uuid "f17c9881-f4b3-5038-8fce-eff3a19f4f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c5ad69f-c453-5e7c-98b1-f37e29e67532" :question/math-academy-id "q-154011" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{y}{4}\\ge -8.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{4}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-8 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{4})\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{4})\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{4}) & \\le 32 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{4}) & \\le 32 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{y}{4} & \\le 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{y}{4} & \\le 32 \\\\ \\frac{4y}{4} & \\le 32 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}y}{\\cancel{4}} & \\le 32 \\\\ y & \\le 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 32.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10739a17-0249-5a0e-8b76-5d07b2aa0ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488507} {:answer/id #uuid "452d066e-6309-536b-a676-51ef94af1656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488611} {:answer/id #uuid "516b5642-52ca-558f-b89f-3669698c909c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488626} {:answer/id #uuid "698410dd-928f-564c-97a4-4166984a33ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488676} {:answer/id #uuid "aab053b1-122b-5e8c-a002-523e16312d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{9}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "516b5642-52ca-558f-b89f-3669698c909c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488626} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef6f073-8e74-5ee7-b25d-e99ceba5ec74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d4bee9d-ce76-579e-9d30-cf5f9cf2d405" :question/math-academy-id "q-6190" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{81}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{1}{81}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{81} & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\times \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{9}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}={9}^{-2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47cde53f-47d7-5ae4-8572-e9ac9bf6754d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47cde53f-47d7-5ae4-8572-e9ac9bf6754d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488613} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a52d3e-493d-5f72-b1e1-ef146d9e54e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83497943-4dd9-5808-bc36-db659744aaad" :question/math-academy-id "q-166797" :question/problem "Expressed as a single fraction, the exact area of the plot of land is$\\,${{field-1}}\n${\\text{mi}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The exact area of land is ${(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}\\,{\\text{mi}}^{2}.$\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{5}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 4}{5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{16}{25}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8803b9-9eda-553f-a752-47d73f4bbb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{4}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488622} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc9514e-15da-5744-b3cb-398a119df9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488623} {:answer/id #uuid "7227bf75-2f02-5620-88b4-37a4f6a8cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488699} {:answer/id #uuid "e75f783f-03f5-5e7e-8e42-cbec60546365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488953} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e80ba-adf6-5413-b1da-31afe0a77c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{4}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7227bf75-2f02-5620-88b4-37a4f6a8cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488699} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5943d0a-4750-5c6b-97ba-3430e1cd7e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "955d0666-e318-54f6-bac2-1bee61b1768a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6194" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{64}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{1}{64}$ as a power with a negative exponent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64} & =\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{4}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}={4}^{-3}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ca890c-4ce7-564d-a6a4-a9bac71abb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=3\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488487} {:answer/id #uuid "5e011c7d-1483-5d78-9bc4-5f179586ff35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=3\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488651} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4faa51-f0ba-59dd-a17c-8673a8ed7588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488926} {:answer/id #uuid "f17c6a34-e689-50a4-8b50-91c915977c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488981} {:answer/id #uuid "fef7ef80-e0c5-5157-bd1a-b6b0059c3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4faa51-f0ba-59dd-a17c-8673a8ed7588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488926} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7ca4c4c-da25-5075-bde9-61051e330507" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7f8c921-7225-588c-b9e8-a78affb7e519" :question/math-academy-id "q-253843" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/05/05737c6e8e7b88f1a91a5fd6cc7d5b6a088227e327ef66e937e279c090a47256.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $3.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{21}{5}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a9cdac2-f621-564f-8709-0203bdf85dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488589} {:answer/id #uuid "60d53825-bf07-5091-90ed-8b078be85249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{873}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488656} {:answer/id #uuid "79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488710} {:answer/id #uuid "7c96d7b0-33e5-5715-9230-bacb2c9fa936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488719} {:answer/id #uuid "ac564bf3-c6f3-5221-811b-35794c5d512f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{323}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488710} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a817628-1149-5ae9-912f-5d3dd1012acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce23a05a-85d4-5790-9eda-0cc10c821050" :question/math-academy-id "q-5650" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c5b5a5c9531b6568c78633f5c44c8332cc722c9d88860a4b6bb9c2c336a1683.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $a$ in the triangle shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=\\frac{15}{2}$ and $c=\\frac{39}{2}.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{39}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\,521}{4}-\\frac{225}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895cc63e-704d-5c75-8834-1c7f86e88688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "895cc63e-704d-5c75-8834-1c7f86e88688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488742} :answer-field/id #uuid "894843a9-487f-53ee-b815-b69de93ad817" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f35643b-d2e7-5b1c-8d2c-7d45128e6a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f35643b-d2e7-5b1c-8d2c-7d45128e6a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488504} :answer-field/id #uuid "9063cade-1a05-55db-bbf9-39633e2bd887" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db6f337c-1879-5d7b-9da6-28f2e468ffa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-352439" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{4x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{31}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $4$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{4x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{4(x+\\frac{3}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $5$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n|  | $5$ | $-\\frac{31}{4}$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-4}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})} & =5-\\frac{(\\frac{31}{4})}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{31}{4x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{4x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-\\frac{31}{4x+3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-31{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4x+3}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[5x-\\frac{31}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+3|]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[5(2-0)-\\frac{31}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|11|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[10-\\frac{31}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{11}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{31}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{11}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c88b39-c18f-5609-af42-c6464e1f7b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488470} {:answer/id #uuid "5562f759-bcb2-503a-ac9b-ecb74fcf42ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488633} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1ae6d0-4bff-533a-a95d-92a0c5ae1908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488754} {:answer/id #uuid "c22dc3fd-3ecc-5018-99e3-cdf1d5738a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488863} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac44af-4e14-559a-9ea7-7a5197b44ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac44af-4e14-559a-9ea7-7a5197b44ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488864} :answer-field/id #uuid "3520d9ec-94dd-56e9-883d-3d54c63b1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe935012-f0c6-58bc-9344-9d20da9c881b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71764" :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{3}{4})$, and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{-2} & ={(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031a974d-9b1e-5723-8d58-6fca1791d2cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{252}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489462} {:answer/id #uuid "127e7c18-d458-55dd-8d52-8b6e1b1d5f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489464} {:answer/id #uuid "6612c30b-4a4f-5379-9362-7887231b0c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489473} {:answer/id #uuid "bb87ab96-2d8e-5e3a-9369-c94d7acaef68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489487} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cfb147-7f8e-5337-9e4c-c8ce4545f759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "127e7c18-d458-55dd-8d52-8b6e1b1d5f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489464} :answer-field/id #uuid "af330894-e3b0-5f96-bf7b-26f02738a953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "308d927b-9585-594c-b5e7-da139c2cdf56" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=24 x - 60 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?" :question/worked-solution "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24 x - 60 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (60 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(24) \\\\ &= 60 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=24+60\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=24+60\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $54.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f7d473-2d4b-516c-9268-0b2806d1a4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{99}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489463} {:answer/id #uuid "2b299f08-d135-5982-959b-fb63b1206a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{231}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489465} {:answer/id #uuid "30332371-e541-5526-ad31-ea0fe696c0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{77}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489466} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc0f155-25cd-54c2-a5ca-25242fdd9010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{99}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489471} {:answer/id #uuid "61d971aa-f63d-5d90-8b35-1be41f05b791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f7d473-2d4b-516c-9268-0b2806d1a4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{99}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489463} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed73cf49-1aff-5c8e-afb5-67724f07dc37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44191e59-3ef1-56f2-b3ae-15156e95a80d" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=22 x - 55 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?" :question/worked-solution "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(22 x - 55 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (55 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(22) \\\\ &= 55 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=22+55\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=22+55\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{99}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $\\frac{99}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e44a42-d9ae-5df3-8081-ebea64b43b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/va